'''''Pleiogynium timoriense''''', commonly known as the '''Burdekin plum''', '''sweet plum''', '''tulip plum''', or in the [[Djabugay]] language '''guybalum''',{{R|TUCKER}} is a medium-sized fruit-bearing tree in the cashew and mango family [[Anacardiaceae]] native to [[Malesia]], [[Australia]] and the [[List of islands in the Pacific Ocean|Pacific Islands]].

==Description==
''Pleiogynium timoriense'' is a  [[semi-deciduous]] rainforest tree growing up to {{convert|20|m|ft|abbr=on}} high in rainforests or around {{convert|12|m|ft|abbr=on}} in cultivation, and may develop [[buttress root]]s on older individuals. It has a dense canopy with glossy dark green leaves up to {{cvt|10|cm}} long by {{cvt|6|cm}} wide, and the trunk has a rough bark. Leaves are [[Glossary of leaf morphology#Leaf structure|imparipinnate]] with 5 to 11 leaflets which are arranged opposite and are elliptic to ovate in shape. The stalk of the terminal leaflet is significantly longer than those of the lateral leaflets. There are prominent domatia on the underside of leaflets.{{R|WILLIAMS}}

The tree is [[Dioecy|dioecious]] (that is, with separate male and female plants) and has yellowish-green  flowers which appear between January and March. The flower calyx lobes are about {{cvt|0.6|–|1|mm}} long, with ovate petals about {{cvt|1.7|-|3.8|mm}} long. Filaments are about {{cvt|1.3|-|2.3|mm}} long.

The fruit is a depressed-obovoid [[drupe]], up to {{cvt|25|by|38|mm}}. The fruit's flesh is generally plum-coloured (dark purple), however, white varieties have been reported. The fruit is edible when ripe.{{R|RFK|LOW}}

==Taxonomy==
The [[basionym]] of this species is ''Icica timoriensis'', and was originally described in ''[[Prodromus Systematis Naturalis Regni Vegetabilis]]'' by [[Augustin Pyramus de Candolle]] in 1825. It was renamed ''Pleiogynium timoriense'' in 1952 by [[Leenh.|Pieter Willem Leenhouts]] in the journal [[Blumea (journal)|Blumea]].{{R|IPNI}} A number of other botanists, notably [[Sol.|Daniel Solander]], [[F.Muell.|Ferdinand von Mueller]] and [[Benth.|George Bentham]], have also described specimens of this species under different names, all of which are now [[Synonym (taxonomy)#Botany|heterotypic synonyms]] of ''Pleiogynium timoriense''{{R|POWO}}

==Etymology==
The genus name ''Pleiogynium'' derives from the Ancient Greek ''[[wikt:πλείων#Ancient Greek|pleíōn]]'' meaning "more", and ''[[wikt:γυνή#Ancient Greek|gunḗ]] ''meaning "female". It refers to the numerous carpels in the flowers. The [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] means "from [[Timor]]", where the first specimen was collected.{{R|COOPER}}

==Distribution==
The natural range of ''P.&nbsp;timoriense'' includes  [[Borneo]], [[Sulawesi]], the [[Philippines]], the [[Moluccas]], [[New Guinea]], the [[Solomon Islands]], the [[Cook Islands]], [[Tonga]], [[Fiji]], [[New Caledonia]], and [[Queensland]].{{R|POWO}} It grows in [[rainforest]] and monsoon forest at elevations from sea level to {{convert|1000|m|ft|abbr=on}}, and is often found along water courses.{{R|RFK|JONES}} In Queensland, the species occurs in dry rainforest, littoral and subcoastal riverine rainforest north from around [[Gympie]].{{R|WILLIAMS}}

==Ecology==
The fruits are eaten by [[Southern Cassowary|cassowaries]] and [[great bowerbird]]s.{{R|COOPER}}

==Uses==
The fruit is edible but tart, although the pale greenish varieties have been found to be more palatable. [[Indigenous Australians]] are known to have buried the fruit underground to ripen. Fruit can be cooked, eaten raw or used in jellies, jams and preserves.{{R|RFK|TUCKER|LOW|JONES}}

[[Joseph Banks]], on his voyage to Australia with Captain [[James Cook]], collected some of the fruits when they stopped in the [[Endeavour River]], and he made the note: "These when gathered off from the tree were very hard and disagreeable but after being kept for a few days became soft and tasted much like indifferent Damsons".{{R|LOW}}

The timber is decorative but seldom used due to the lack of supply of good logs.{{R|RFK}}

